conn: lay out client Initial packets like Chrome's chaos protection

Chrome (quiche's QuicChaosProtector) splits the CRYPTO data of an Initial
packet at random, adds PING frames, spreads the padding over the packet as
several PADDING frames and shuffles all frames, so the packet does not end
with one block of padding. A ClientHello needing two packets is shared out
first (QuicPacketCreator::MultiPacketChaosProtect): the first packet carries
its first 55 to 86 bytes and its end, the second the middle, and both about
as much padding. The packets stay as large as before; 0-RTT packets no
longer share the datagram of an Initial packet, as with Chrome.

